          "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nLet <MATH_0001> be a scheme. An <TEXTITOPEN_0001>elliptic curve<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> over <MATH_0002> is a triple\n<MATH_0003> where <MATH_0004> is a scheme and <MATH_0005> and <MATH_0006>\nare morphisms of schemes such that\n<STRUCT_0002>\n<STRUCT_0003> <MATH_0007> is proper, smooth of relative dimension <MATH_0008>,\n<STRUCT_0004> for every <MATH_0009> the fibre <MATH_0010> is a connected curve\nof genus <MATH_0011>, i.e., <MATH_0012> and <MATH_0013>\nboth are <MATH_0014>-dimensional <MATH_0015>-vector spaces, and\n<STRUCT_0005> <MATH_0016> is a section of <MATH_0017>.\n<STRUCT_0006>\nGiven elliptic curves <MATH_0018> and <MATH_0019> a\n<TEXTITOPEN_0002>morphism of elliptic curves over <MATH_0020><TEXTITCLOSE_0002>\nis a morphism <MATH_0021> such that the diagram\n<MATH_0022>\nis commutative and the inner square is cartesian, in other words the\nmorphism <MATH_0023> induces an isomorphism <MATH_0024>.\nWe are going to define the stack of elliptic curves <MATH_0025>.\nIn the rest of the Stacks project we work out the method introduced in\nDeligne and Mumford's paper <REF_0001> which consists in presenting\n<MATH_0026> as a category endowed with a functor\n<MATH_0027>\nThis means you work with fibred categories over the categories of schemes,\ntopologies, stacks fibred in groupoids, coverings, etc, etc.\nIn this chapter we throw all of that out of the window and\nwe think about it a bit differently -- probably closer to how the initiators\nof the theory started thinking about it themselves.\n\n\n",
